# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-enquiry-4.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2 has nothing from it; hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.3 have "qualities_in_mind → contra_reason"; they differ when only "it is a principle of reason that all sensible qualities are in the mind, not in the object" is true, which the first allows
hume-enquiry-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "it is a principle of reason that all sensible qualities are in the mind, not in the object" is true and "the opinion of external existence is contrary to reason" is false. Is S2, "The second objection goes farther, and represents this opinion as contrary to reason: at least, if it be a principle of reason, that all sensible qualities are in the mind, not in the object.", true then?
hume-enquiry-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2 has "bereft → annihilated ∧ not_worth"; hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.3 have "bereft → annihilated" and "annihilated → not_worth"; they differ when only "matter is in a manner annihilated, leaving only a certain unknown, inexplicable something as the cause of our perceptions" is true, which the first allows
hume-enquiry-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2, to: Suppose "matter is in a manner annihilated, leaving only a certain unknown, inexplicable something as the cause of our perceptions" is true and "matter is bereaved of all its intelligible qualities, both primary and secondary" and "the notion of matter is so imperfect that no sceptic will think it worth while to contend against it" are false. Is S3, "Bereave matter of all its intelligible qualities, both primary and secondary, you in a manner annihilate it, and leave only a certain unknown, inexplicable something, as the cause of our perceptions; a notion so imperfect, that no sceptic will think it worth while to contend against it.", true then?
hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2 has "qualities_in_mind → bereft" and "annihilated → contra_reason"; hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "the opinion of external existence is contrary to reason" and "it is a principle of reason that all sensible qualities are in the mind, not in the object" are true, which the second allows
hume-enquiry-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2 has "qualities_in_mind → contra_reason"; hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.3 have "bereft → not_worth"; they differ when only "the opinion of external existence is contrary to reason", "it is a principle of reason that all sensible qualities are in the mind, not in the object" and "matter is bereaved of all its intelligible qualities, both primary and secondary" are true, which the first allows
hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2 puts it at S2, "The second objection goes farther, and represents this opinion as contrary to reason: at least, if it be a principle of reason, that all sensible qualities are in the mind, not in the object."; hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.2 and hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2.3 at S3, "Bereave matter of all its intelligible qualities, both primary and secondary, you in a manner annihilate it, and leave only a certain unknown, inexplicable something, as the cause of our perceptions; a notion so imperfect, that no sceptic will think it worth while to contend against it."
hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps hume-enquiry-4.logic.r2, to: Is the text's main conclusion S2, "The second objection goes farther, and represents this opinion as contrary to reason: at least, if it be a principle of reason, that all sensible qualities are in the mind, not in the object.", rather than S3, "Bereave matter of all its intelligible qualities, both primary and secondary, you in a manner annihilate it, and leave only a certain unknown, inexplicable something, as the cause of our perceptions; a notion so imperfect, that no sceptic will think it worth while to contend against it."?
